WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.390
ED GLAESER: I thought we should start with the Zipcar story.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:05.540
How did you come up with this idea?

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:06.980
How did all this happen?

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.410
ROBIN CHASE: Zipcar is today the largest car-sharing company in the world.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:15.060
And this was back in the fall of '99 that we were founded.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:17.257
What's interesting to reflect on is this was

00:00:17.257 --> 00:00:18.840
an idea that came from someplace else.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.580
This was an idea that was being executed in Europe since about the mid-'80s.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:23.870
And my co-founder is German.

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:25.460
She was sitting in a cafe.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.605
She looked across the street and saw a shared car.

00:00:27.605 --> 00:00:30.095
And she came back to Cambridge and said, what do you think of this?

00:00:30.095 --> 00:00:31.350
GLAESER: So she was in what city?

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:31.900
CHASE: Berlin.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:32.720
GLAESER: She was in Berlin.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.490
CHASE: In Berlin, sitting in a cafe.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.890
And she asked me, because I had a business background.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:39.260
And I'm a very honest person.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.710
But I've told this story now so many times it feels apocryphal,

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.110
but I think it's true that really a light bulb went on over my head,

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:47.150
and I said, oh, my god.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:53.090
This is what the internet is made for, sharing very specific resources easily

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:54.320
among lots of people.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.040
This is what wireless is made for, getting that reservation from a server

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.590
directly to the car.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.280
Remember, this is the fall of '99.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.320
And this is what I personally want, because I

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:04.902
had three children and a husband.

00:01:04.902 --> 00:01:06.860
And I lived in a dense metro area with one car.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.210
And my husband would take that car, and there was no way in hell--

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.240
GLAESER: And sometimes you'd need to use a car, right?

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:11.990
CHASE: And I needed a car sometimes.

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:16.220
And I didn't want to park it, maintain it, snow shovel it, any of that.

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:17.990
But I needed a car sometimes.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.520
And so it really did snap together that this is precisely what I want.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.270
GLAESER: When you were rolling out Zipcar,

00:01:23.270 --> 00:01:25.978
how did you think about which markets would be the most receptive

00:01:25.978 --> 00:01:27.490
and which ones would be problematic?

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.990
CHASE: What's amusing about that question is I've also

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.834
been asked why did you start in Boston?

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:35.000
And I started in Boston because I lived here, right?

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.560
There's no fancy market analysis that went into that.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.950
But as we rolled out, and even within the city--

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:41.070
GLAESER: Although, there was something inherent in it, right?

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:44.153
Because you thought of Zipcar because you lived there and because you knew

00:01:44.153 --> 00:01:45.860
it would be a good match for your life.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:46.818
CHASE: It's true.

00:01:46.818 --> 00:01:47.720
And so I did--

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:50.870
absolutely correct in that I think I was the right person

00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:54.320
to hear that story at the right time, because I was a user,

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.010
and I lived that environment.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:58.310
So if we think about even expanding within Boston

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.640
and to the next cities, what we learned is

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:07.100
that Zipcar works in places where people don't need a car to get to work.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:12.692
And it works best where the price of parking is very high per month.

00:02:12.692 --> 00:02:15.650
GLAESER: Now, one side of Zipcar is, of course, cutting-edge technology

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:16.120
and totally now.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.203
And the other sense is playing into something that

00:02:18.203 --> 00:02:19.940
is as old as the oldest cities, right?

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.580
I mean, the sharing economy is not new to urban spaces, right?

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.340
I mean, what is an urban restaurant other than a shared kitchen, a shared

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:25.560
dining room, what is--

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.520
CHASE: I'm laughing because you are saying exactly what I tell people

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.061
all the time.

00:02:29.061 --> 00:02:32.387
Because they keep saying to me, oh, no, sharing this cultural things,

00:02:32.387 --> 00:02:33.470
you do it in your country.

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.428
You do it in your city.

00:02:34.428 --> 00:02:36.120
But in our country, in our city--

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.870
and I say, you're crazy you've been sharing since time immemorial.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:39.590
Exactly.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.440
I'm completely in agreement with you.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.400
GLAESER: So I thought about Zipcar when I was a kid in New York in the 1970s.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.564
And could you have had it?

00:02:47.564 --> 00:02:49.355
And one reason why you couldn't have had it

00:02:49.355 --> 00:02:51.396
is the technological issues in terms of doing it.

00:02:51.396 --> 00:02:54.200
But the other issue is the issue of safety

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.034
and would the car be OK when you got it?

00:02:56.034 --> 00:02:57.950
So I've long had this sort of fantasy of going

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:00.620
to pick up a Zipcar in Times Square in 1977

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.360
and finding a dead body in the trunk.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.735
And that would make the whole experience fairly negative.

00:03:04.735 --> 00:03:07.943
Now, of course, with the technology, you now know who had the car beforehand.

00:03:07.943 --> 00:03:09.140
It would all be fairly easy.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.723
But in some sense, I tend to think of it as the technology has

00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:14.010
enabled us to share more stuff.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.020
CHASE: Completely.

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.490
So I wrote this book, Peers Inc. And when I was writing the book,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.160
I had to come up with what were the specific things that

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.660
were enabling this kind of sharing.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:27.170
And I would say precise that technology has

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:31.760
made the transaction of sharing, of dealing with small individual parts,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.340
trivial.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.920
So finding those individual things, paying for those individual things,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.640
getting access like Zipcar, like a car just really fast.

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:47.360
And importantly, this ability to move from trusting friends and trusting

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.420
relatives to trusting the next circle out

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:52.340
of friends of friends and strangers.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:55.040
And that is enabled by technology, totally

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.071
new today with the ability to do ratings and commentaries.

00:04:00.071 --> 00:04:01.820
And that is something that has transformed

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:03.202
even since we started Zipcar.

00:04:03.202 --> 00:04:04.910
And I think that's one of the things that

00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:07.370
has made the sharing economy blossom.

00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:10.027
People talk about the gig economy and the platform economy

00:04:10.027 --> 00:04:12.110
and the sharing economy and the on-demand economy.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.750
And for me, all of those names work.

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.100
But this book that I wrote was to call out the fact

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.290
that when we talk about those, we're actually

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.831
talking about this brand new collaboration, this partnership,

00:04:23.831 --> 00:04:28.520
between a large entity that I'm calling the Inc that is doing all

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:30.110
the things that big companies do well.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.550
So the gig economy is forgetting that it's only enabled by the platforms.

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:37.370
And the platform economy is nothing, zero,

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.610
if it didn't have this myriad of diversity around it.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:41.750
So it's this brand new collaboration that

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:45.950
for me, is what is exciting and thrilling about the future

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:50.240
and enables us to get the best of these both worlds.

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:54.980
And if I think about what I love about this particular future vision

00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:01.450
of the city is it's really celebrating, celebrating and getting

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:05.290
value out of people's individual uniqueness.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.020
And so it's a delightful, amazing thing.

00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.020
I feel like we each have our own side to play,

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:17.040
and that is enabled by the internet that now makes this possible.

